// src/user/input_server/seat_kind.pdx -- Wave0-B8-06 (paideia-os #2285)
//
// KIND_SEAT = 0x1D6, derived over KIND_INPUT_SERVER (0x1D0, landed by
// sibling row B8-01 in this same Wave-0 Batch-8 dispatch).
//
// Design authority:
//   * design/compositor/pwp-spec-vocabulary.md §2.9 KIND_SEAT -- one
//     collection of input devices bound to one logical user session
//     (Wayland's seat vocabulary). Carries keyboard + pointer + touch +
//     stylus device slots and an active-workspace binding. Seat identity
//     is what KIND_INPUT_ROUTE (§2.10) binds against, and what seat
//     lock/unlock (G8.M5-002, #2286) mediates for the emergency-console
//     handover flow.
//   * design/compositor/pwp-spec-vocabulary.md §3 derivation graph --
//     KIND_SEAT hangs off KIND_IPC_ENDPOINT in the abstract graph, but
//     the concrete substrate parent is KIND_INPUT_SERVER, which owns the
//     seat table and is the only authority that may mint seats. This
//     mirrors how KIND_LAYER derives from KIND_WINDOW (its concrete
//     substrate parent) rather than directly from KIND_SURFACE.
//   * design/roadmap/next-wave-synthesis.md §4 pitfall P2 -- the input
//     server is a compositor-INDEPENDENT process. A locked seat blocks
//     event dispatch AT THE SEAT ROW, not at every subscriber; the
//     screen-lock decision is a single capability operation, not an
//     ambient global state a hundred subscribers each have to consult.
//   * design/roadmap/next-wave-softarch.md line 649 tail sketch --
//     {seat_id:u8, capabilities_bitmap:u16, active_focus_slot:u8}. This
//     landing widens seat_id to u32 (dense-packed monotonic minting
//     across a multi-user session; u8's 256 ceiling is nonsensical when
//     a login-multiplexing kiosk chain can produce 500+ seat IDs in a
//     work day) and replaces the capabilities_bitmap with typed device
//     kind_id slots. Rationale: a bitmap forces every consumer to keep
//     a parallel table of "which u16-bit means which device kind"; typed
//     slots let the seat row name its bound devices directly, and the
//     "capabilities" question devolves into "which slots are nonzero".
//     Grepping for "capabilities_bitmap" across svc-compositor and the
//     input server yields zero consumers -- the sketch was aspirational.
//
// PURPOSE. A derived-kind capability that names one physical user (one
// seat in the multi-seat sense). Owns:
//   * one KIND_KEYBOARD_DEVICE cap  (required at mint)
//   * one KIND_POINTER_DEVICE cap   (required at mint)
//   * one KIND_TOUCH_DEVICE cap     (optional; 0 = no touch, added
//                                    later via seat_bind_touch)
//   * one KIND_STYLUS_DEVICE cap    (optional; 0 = no stylus, added
//                                    later via seat_bind_stylus)
//   * a screen-locked flag          (P2 dispatch-refusal gate)
//   * an active-workspace binding   (which KIND_WORKSPACE the seat
//                                    is currently viewing)
//
// A typical desktop is one seat. A shared-workstation kiosk chain
// (fitness center, library terminal room) is N seats, each with its
// own bound keyboard + pointer + display group. A multi-seat gaming
// rig sharing one machine is N seats, one per player. The seat is
// where "who is this input event for" is answered; KIND_INPUT_ROUTE
// (§2.10) is the linear cap the input server mints per focus decision
// to name the (seat, surface) pair the next event flows through.
//
// =============================================================================
// NOVEL-CLEAN CHOICES
// =============================================================================
//
// (1) SEAT LOCKING IS A FIRST-CLASS RIGHT, NOT AN AMBIENT STATE.
//     The screen-lock decision is a capability operation: hold
//     RIGHT_LOCK, invoke seat_lock, done. Holding RIGHT_UNLOCK is what
//     lets the lock-screen agent (or a session-close hook) release the
//     seat again. A process that lacks RIGHT_LOCK cannot lock the seat
//     even if it can send events into it. This is the POSIX/X11 posture
//     turned inside out: X11 grabs the keyboard by protocol, and every
//     window server has re-implemented some form of "am I locked right
//     now" ambient flag that clients query out-of-band. Here the flag
//     lives on the seat row, and the dispatch check (P2 mitigation) is
//     one byte load at the seat, not a broadcast to every subscriber.
//
// (2) LOCKED SEATS BLOCK DISPATCH AT THE SEAT, NOT AT SUBSCRIBERS.
//     The input server's event-dispatch inner loop reads the locked
//     byte at seat_row + 32 before it fans events out to KIND_INPUT_
//     ROUTE consumers. If locked == 1 the events are dropped (or, per
//     the recovery-plane flow #2270, redirected to the lock screen);
//     no per-subscriber "am I allowed to receive this" check is
//     needed. The one gate is the seat.
//
// (3) DEVICE BINDING IS TYPED, NOT BITMAP-INDEXED.
//     The row carries typed kind_id slots (keyboard_kind_id, pointer_
//     kind_id, touch_kind_id, stylus_kind_id) rather than a
//     capabilities_bitmap indexed by a parallel enum. This is the
//     RECORD-versus-TAGGED-UNION posture the KIND_WINDOW landing (§2.4,
//     B6-05, #2254) takes for its parent/z/tab/workspace/a11y slots:
//     one field per named authority, no separate "which bit means
//     which" reconciliation.
//
// (4) ACTIVE-WORKSPACE IS A SEAT-LOCAL BINDING.
//     A multi-seat workstation lets each seat name its OWN current
//     workspace. The active_workspace_kind_id lives on the seat row so
//     seat A viewing workspace 3 while seat B views workspace 5 is the
//     structural default, not a compositor policy hack.
//
// =============================================================================
// SLOT ALLOCATION -- KIND_SEAT = 0x1D6
// =============================================================================
//
// Consulted design/architecture/next-wave-derived-kinds.md and grepped
// src/ for 0x1D6 -- unclaimed at landing. Neighbours:
//   0x1D0  KIND_INPUT_SERVER            (B8-01 sibling, in-parallel)
//   0x1D1  RP_COMPOSITOR_KIND_FORWARD   (recovery-plane forward kind)
//   0x1D2..0x1D5  reserved for B8-02..05 device kinds
//                 (KIND_KEYBOARD_DEVICE, KIND_POINTER_DEVICE,
//                  KIND_TOUCH_DEVICE, KIND_STYLUS_DEVICE)
//   0x1D6  KIND_SEAT                    (B8-06, THIS FILE)
//   0x1D7..0x1DF  reserved for later B8 rows (KIND_INPUT_ROUTE,
//                 KIND_HOTKEY_BINDING, ...)
//
// If B8-01 lands KIND_INPUT_SERVER at a slot other than 0x1D0, the
// SEAT_PARENT_KIND constant below is the single reconciliation point
// (one-line edit + fingerprint re-emission). The recovery-plane
// landings already pin 0x1D0 as RESERVATION_HOLDER_KIND (see
// src/user/compositor/recovery_plane_reserve.pdx line 203), which
// pre-committed the slot to the input server before this landing.
//
// =============================================================================
// ROW LAYOUT -- 64 BYTES (ONE CACHE LINE)
// =============================================================================
//
//   [+0]  header:   in_use[63:56] | reserved[55:0]
//   [+8]  ids1:     seat_id[31:0]          | keyboard_kind_id[63:32]
//   [+16] ids2:     pointer_kind_id[31:0]  | touch_kind_id[63:32]
//   [+24] ids3:     stylus_kind_id[31:0]   | active_workspace_kind_id[63:32]
//   [+32] state:    locked[7:0]            | reserved[63:8]
//   [+40] reserved: (future: cursor_theme_kind_id | keymap_kind_id)
//   [+48] reserved: (future: xkb_state_kind_id  | ime_router_kind_id)
//   [+56] reserved: (future: parent-seat edge for seat-groups)
//
// All slots u32 little-endian. locked is one byte (0 or 1) at the low
// byte of the state word; the dispatch check is a single byte load,
// no unpack needed. All reserved words zero at mint; a future landing
// that widens the row must not shrink this file's zero-write shape.
//
// =============================================================================
// FAILURE TAXONOMY -- 0xFFFFEB70..0xFFFFEB7F
// =============================================================================
//
// Fresh 16-slot band verified at landing time by grep across
// src/**/*.pdx. Neighbours:
//   0xFFFFEB40..4F  KIND_FRAMEBUFFER
//   0xFFFFEB50..5F  KIND_KEK
//   0xFFFFEB60..6F  KIND_HOTPLUG_CHANNEL
//   0xFFFFEB70..7F  KIND_SEAT           (THIS BAND)
//   0xFFFFEB80..DF  entirely free (next fresh band for later Wave-0 rows)
//
// The row spec's suggested band (0xFFFFEB50..5F) is taken by KIND_KEK;
// we advance to the next contiguous free page per the grep-verify
// discipline the row itself demands.

module SeatKind = structure {

  // ==========================================================================
  // SECTION 1 -- KIND ORDINAL + PARENT + POOL CEILING
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let KIND_SEAT        : u64 = 0x1D6
  pub let SEAT_PARENT_KIND : u64 = 0x1D0   // KIND_INPUT_SERVER (B8-01 sibling)

  // Pool ceiling. 8 concurrent seats -- a typical desktop is 1, a
  // shared-workstation kiosk chain rarely exceeds 4, and 8 covers the
  // multi-seat gaming rig case with margin. 8 * 64 = 512 bytes -- one
  // half-page slab.
  pub let SEAT_MAX       : u64 = 8
  pub let SEAT_ROW_BYTES : u64 = 64

  // Common u32 mask (documentary only -- the assembly uses shl 32 /
  // shr 32 for u32 zero-extension per this file's encoder-conservative
  // posture; no `and reg, imm64` and no `mov reg, 0xFFFFFFFF` reliance
  // on ambiguous zero-vs-sign-extend paths).
  pub let SEAT_U32_MAX : u64 = 0xFFFFFFFF

  // ==========================================================================
  // SECTION 2 -- RIGHTS
  // ==========================================================================
  //
  // Seven rights, each addressable independently. The lock/unlock
  // split is a novel-clean choice (see NOVEL-CLEAN §1 above): a
  // process that can UNLOCK need not be able to LOCK, and vice versa.
  // The lock-screen agent holds RIGHT_UNLOCK; a session-close hook
  // holds RIGHT_LOCK; the seat itself never holds either (a locked
  // seat cannot unlock itself).
  //
  //   RIGHT_QUERY_DEVICES     -- read the bound device kind_ids
  //   RIGHT_LOCK              -- transition locked 0 -> 1
  //   RIGHT_UNLOCK            -- transition locked 1 -> 0
  //   RIGHT_MINT_INPUT_ROUTE  -- mint a KIND_INPUT_ROUTE naming this
  //                              seat (the input server holds this
  //                              right on every seat; svc-compositor
  //                              does not)
  //   RIGHT_OBSERVE           -- introspection: seat_id, locked flag,
  //                              active workspace (no device kind_ids)
  //   R_SEAT_MINT             -- derive a child kind (future; e.g. a
  //                              per-seat KIND_HOTKEY_BINDING)
  //   R_SEAT_REVOKE           -- revoke the seat and cascade to its
  //                              bound devices

  pub let RIGHT_QUERY_DEVICES     : u64 = 0x001
  pub let RIGHT_LOCK              : u64 = 0x002
  pub let RIGHT_UNLOCK            : u64 = 0x004
  pub let RIGHT_MINT_INPUT_ROUTE  : u64 = 0x008
  pub let R_SEAT_REVOKE           : u64 = 0x010
  pub let R_SEAT_MINT             : u64 = 0x200
  pub let RIGHT_OBSERVE           : u64 = 0x400
  pub let R_SEAT_ALL              : u64 = 0x61F
     // 0x001 | 0x002 | 0x004 | 0x008 | 0x010 | 0x200 | 0x400

  // ==========================================================================
  // SECTION 3 -- ROW LAYOUT OFFSETS
  // ==========================================================================
  //
  // Machine-readable spelling of the row layout documented in the
  // file header. Every producer / consumer reads through THESE
  // constants, not through hand-rolled +8 arithmetic.

  pub let SEAT_OFF_HEADER    : u64 = 0
  pub let SEAT_OFF_IDS1      : u64 = 8    // seat_id | keyboard_kind_id
  pub let SEAT_OFF_IDS2      : u64 = 16   // pointer_kind_id | touch_kind_id
  pub let SEAT_OFF_IDS3      : u64 = 24   // stylus_kind_id | active_workspace_kind_id
  pub let SEAT_OFF_STATE     : u64 = 32   // locked byte at [+32]
  pub let SEAT_OFF_RESERVED1 : u64 = 40
  pub let SEAT_OFF_RESERVED2 : u64 = 48
  pub let SEAT_OFF_RESERVED3 : u64 = 56

  // Sub-word offset for the locked byte -- targeted byte-load path.
  pub let SEAT_STATE_LOCKED_BYTE : u64 = 32   // = SEAT_OFF_STATE + 0

  // Locked-flag values.
  pub let SEAT_LOCKED_NO  : u64 = 0
  pub let SEAT_LOCKED_YES : u64 = 1

  // ==========================================================================
  // SECTION 4 -- OP TAGS
  // ==========================================================================
  //
  // Kernel dispatch surface. Every op is arity-one from the caller's
  // perspective; op_arg semantics vary by op. Dispatch lands in a
  // follow-up milestone (G8.M5-002, #2286 for lock/unlock; G8.M5-003
  // for device binding; G8.M6-* for input-route mint).

  pub let SEAT_OP_QUERY_ID              : u64 = 0
  pub let SEAT_OP_QUERY_KEYBOARD        : u64 = 1
  pub let SEAT_OP_QUERY_POINTER         : u64 = 2
  pub let SEAT_OP_QUERY_TOUCH           : u64 = 3
  pub let SEAT_OP_QUERY_STYLUS          : u64 = 4
  pub let SEAT_OP_QUERY_LOCKED          : u64 = 5
  pub let SEAT_OP_QUERY_ACTIVE_WORKSPACE: u64 = 6
  pub let SEAT_OP_BIND_TOUCH            : u64 = 7   // op_arg = touch_kind_id
  pub let SEAT_OP_BIND_STYLUS           : u64 = 8   // op_arg = stylus_kind_id
  pub let SEAT_OP_LOCK                  : u64 = 9
  pub let SEAT_OP_UNLOCK                : u64 = 10
  pub let SEAT_OP_SET_ACTIVE_WORKSPACE  : u64 = 11  // op_arg = workspace_kind_id
  pub let SEAT_OP_DEBUG_PRINT           : u64 = 12  // R_SEAT_ALL & OBSERVE
  pub let SEAT_OP_MAX                   : u64 = 12

  // ==========================================================================
  // SECTION 5 -- FAILURE TAXONOMY -- 0xFFFFEB70..0xFFFFEB7F
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let SEAT_OK                    : u64 = 0
  pub let SEAT_TAIL_ENOSPC           : u64 = 0xFFFFEB7F
  pub let SEAT_MINT_BAD_ID           : u64 = 0xFFFFEB7E
  pub let SEAT_MINT_BAD_KEYBOARD     : u64 = 0xFFFFEB7D
  pub let SEAT_MINT_BAD_POINTER      : u64 = 0xFFFFEB7C
  pub let SEAT_MINT_BAD_RIGHTS       : u64 = 0xFFFFEB7B
  pub let SEAT_BIND_BAD_ID           : u64 = 0xFFFFEB7A
  pub let SEAT_BIND_ALREADY          : u64 = 0xFFFFEB79
  pub let SEAT_LOCK_ALREADY          : u64 = 0xFFFFEB78
  pub let SEAT_UNLOCK_ALREADY        : u64 = 0xFFFFEB77
  pub let SEAT_BAD_SLOT              : u64 = 0xFFFFEB76
  pub let SEAT_BAD_RIGHTS            : u64 = 0xFFFFEB75
  pub let SEAT_WRONG_KIND            : u64 = 0xFFFFEB74
  pub let SEAT_REVOKE_ALREADY        : u64 = 0xFFFFEB73
  pub let SEAT_UNSUP_OP              : u64 = 0xFFFFEB72
  pub let SEAT_BAD_ARG               : u64 = 0xFFFFEB71
  pub let SEAT_ROW_NONE              : u64 = 0xFFFFEB70

  // ==========================================================================
  // SECTION 6 -- STATS COUNTER SLOTS
  // ==========================================================================
  //
  // The substrate will publish an _sk_stats [u64; 8] table in a follow-
  // up milestone; slot indices named here so cross-module bumpers agree.

  pub let SEAT_ST_MINTS         : u64 = 0
  pub let SEAT_ST_REVOKES       : u64 = 1
  pub let SEAT_ST_LOCKS         : u64 = 2   // successful lock transitions
  pub let SEAT_ST_UNLOCKS       : u64 = 3   // successful unlock transitions
  pub let SEAT_ST_BIND_TOUCH    : u64 = 4
  pub let SEAT_ST_BIND_STYLUS   : u64 = 5
  pub let SEAT_ST_REFUSED       : u64 = 6
  pub let SEAT_ST_MAX           : u64 = 8

  // ==========================================================================
  // SECTION 7 -- FINGERPRINT (one line, legacy-suffixed)
  // ==========================================================================
  //
  // Emitted once on successful mint by the future substrate. The
  // legacy-suffix convention is what tools/verify-fingerprint-coverage.sh
  // enumerates, so this literal is the machine-checkable name of the
  // milestone. No fingerprint on bind / lock / unlock / query paths.
  //
  // Byte count: "seat mint ok [legacy: SEAT MINT OK]\0" = 35 chars + NUL = 36.
  pub let pdx_kind_seat_meta : [u8; 36] = "seat mint ok [legacy: SEAT MINT OK]\0"

  // ==========================================================================
  // SECTION 8 -- VALIDATORS (pure leaves)
  // ==========================================================================
  //
  // Every function in this section is a pure unsafe-bodied leaf with
  // small immediates, returns in rax, and clobbers only rax + one
  // scratch. No `test`, no `and reg, imm64`. Labels prefixed seat_.

  // seat_id_valid(id) -> 1 iff id in (0, 2^32).
  // seat_id == 0 is refused so the row's header can use zero as the
  // "no live seat" sentinel without ambiguity.
  pub let seat_id_valid : (u64) -> u64 !{} @{} =
    fn (id: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "B8-06 (#2285): seat_id validator. 1 iff id in (0, 2^32). Zero is the row-empty sentinel; upper 32 bits must be zero so the packed ids1 word cannot smear seat_id into keyboard_kind_id. Uses shr 32 rather than `and reg, 0xFFFFFFFF` per this file's encoder-conservative posture. Labels prefixed seat_iv_.",
      block: {
        cmp rdi, 0;
        je  seat_iv_no;
        mov rax, rdi;
        shr rax, 32;
        cmp rax, 0;
        jne seat_iv_no;
        mov rax, 1;
        ret;
      seat_iv_no:
        xor rax, rax;
        ret
      }
    }

  // seat_device_kind_valid(kind_id) -> 1 iff kind_id in (0, 2^32).
  // Zero is refused (a zero kind_id means "no device", which is
  // handled at bind time via a different sentinel path -- the mint
  // gate requires a live keyboard + pointer).
  pub let seat_device_kind_valid : (u64) -> u64 !{} @{} =
    fn (kind_id: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "B8-06 (#2285): device kind_id validator (nonzero + fits u32). Uses shr 32 to check upper bits are clear rather than `and reg, 0xFFFFFFFF`. Labels prefixed seat_dv_.",
      block: {
        cmp rdi, 0;
        je  seat_dv_no;
        mov rax, rdi;
        shr rax, 32;
        cmp rax, 0;
        jne seat_dv_no;
        mov rax, 1;
        ret;
      seat_dv_no:
        xor rax, rax;
        ret
      }
    }

  // seat_workspace_kind_valid(kind_id) -> 1 iff kind_id in [0, 2^32).
  // Zero IS legal at seat-mint time (a fresh seat has no workspace
  // binding yet; the compositor's default-workspace-assignment hook
  // fills this in on first present).
  pub let seat_workspace_kind_valid : (u64) -> u64 !{} @{} =
    fn (kind_id: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "B8-06 (#2285): workspace kind_id validator (fits u32; zero legal). Uses shr 32 rather than `and reg, 0xFFFFFFFF`. Labels prefixed seat_wv_.",
      block: {
        mov rax, rdi;
        shr rax, 32;
        cmp rax, 0;
        jne seat_wv_no;
        mov rax, 1;
        ret;
      seat_wv_no:
        xor rax, rax;
        ret
      }
    }

  // seat_rights_valid(rights) -> 1 iff rights subset of R_SEAT_ALL.
  pub let seat_rights_valid : (u64) -> u64 !{} @{} =
    fn (rights: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "B8-06 (#2285): rights subset test against R_SEAT_ALL = 0x61F. Small immediate, so `and reg, imm32` sign-extends 0x61F to 0x000000000000061F -- correct as a mask. Labels prefixed seat_rv_.",
      block: {
        mov rax, rdi;
        mov r10, 0x61F;
        and rax, r10;
        cmp rax, rdi;
        jne seat_rv_no;
        mov rax, 1;
        ret;
      seat_rv_no:
        xor rax, rax;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // SECTION 9 -- PACKERS
  // ==========================================================================
  //
  // Every packer takes two u32 fields (interpreted as low + high halves
  // of one packed u64) and returns the packed word. Fields are masked
  // to 32 bits via shl 32 / shr 32 so an out-of-range argument cannot
  // smear into a neighbour. No `and reg, imm64`, no `mov reg, 0xFFFFFFFF`
  // reliance.

  // seat_pack32(low, high) -> low[31:0] | high[63:32].
  // Generic u32 packer used by ids1 / ids2 / ids3 writes.
  pub let seat_pack32 : (u64, u64) -> u64 !{} @{} =
    fn (low: u64) (high: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "B8-06 (#2285): pack two u32 fields into one u64 as low[31:0] | high[63:32]. Both fields masked via shl 32 / shr 32 (low) and shl 32 (high) so out-of-range arguments cannot smear. No `and reg, imm64` per this file's encoder-conservative posture. Labels prefixed seat_p32_.",
      block: {
        // Mask low arg to 32 bits: shl 32 then shr 32.
        mov rax, rdi;
        shl rax, 32;
        shr rax, 32;
        // Move high arg into high 32 bits of a scratch.
        mov r10, rsi;
        shl r10, 32;
        or  rax, r10;
        ret
      }
    }

  // seat_hdr_pack() -> canonical row header. Only in_use is ever
  // written (by tail_alloc, not here); low 56 bits stay 0. Mirrors
  // xdg_shell_states_hdr_pack.
  pub let seat_hdr_pack : () -> u64 !{} @{} =
    fn () -> unsafe {
      effects: {}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "B8-06 (#2285): canonical row header. Only in_use is written (by tail_alloc); low 56 bits stay 0. Mirrors xdg_shell_states_hdr_pack. Labels prefixed seat_hp_.",
      block: {
        xor rax, rax;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // SECTION 10 -- FACTORY: seat_mint(row_ptr, id, keyboard, pointer)
  // ==========================================================================
  //
  // Writes seat_id + keyboard + pointer into an already-allocated row,
  // clears touch/stylus/locked/active_workspace to zero, and returns
  // SEAT_OK. Does NOT allocate a row (that is the mint gate's job)
  // and does NOT bump stats counters (the substrate's dispatch handler
  // owns those). Validates seat_id + keyboard + pointer; refuses on
  // failure and leaves the row untouched.
  //
  // Signature: (row_ptr: u64, seat_id: u64, keyboard: u64, pointer: u64) -> u64
  //   * row_ptr:  pointer to a 64-byte row already tail-allocated
  //   * seat_id:  dense-packed monotonic (u32, nonzero)
  //   * keyboard: KIND_KEYBOARD_DEVICE kind_id (u32, nonzero)
  //   * pointer:  KIND_POINTER_DEVICE kind_id  (u32, nonzero)
  //   * returns:  SEAT_OK on success; SEAT_MINT_BAD_ID / _BAD_KEYBOARD /
  //               _BAD_POINTER on validator failure. Row is untouched
  //               on failure.
  pub let seat_mint : (u64, u64, u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (row_ptr: u64) (seat_id: u64) (keyboard: u64) (pointer: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "B8-06 (#2285): factory writing seat_id + keyboard + pointer + zeroed touch/stylus/locked/active_workspace into an existing row. Validation order: seat_id nonzero + fits u32 (SEAT_MINT_BAD_ID); keyboard nonzero + fits u32 (SEAT_MINT_BAD_KEYBOARD); pointer nonzero + fits u32 (SEAT_MINT_BAD_POINTER). All validations complete before any store, so a failed mint leaves the row byte-identical to its pre-call state. Scratch: rax, r8, r9, r10, r11 (all SysV caller-saved). No callee-save touches. All u32 masking via shl 32 / shr 32 -- no `and reg, imm64`. Labels prefixed seat_m_.",
      block: {
        // ---- Validate seat_id (nonzero, fits u32). ----
        cmp rsi, 0;
        je  seat_m_bad_id;
        mov r10, rsi;
        shr r10, 32;
        cmp r10, 0;
        jne seat_m_bad_id;

        // ---- Validate keyboard (nonzero, fits u32). ----
        cmp rdx, 0;
        je  seat_m_bad_kb;
        mov r10, rdx;
        shr r10, 32;
        cmp r10, 0;
        jne seat_m_bad_kb;

        // ---- Validate pointer (nonzero, fits u32). ----
        cmp rcx, 0;
        je  seat_m_bad_ptr;
        mov r10, rcx;
        shr r10, 32;
        cmp r10, 0;
        jne seat_m_bad_ptr;

        // ---- All validated. Commit the writes. ----
        // header ([+0]): zero (in_use is set by tail_alloc, not here).
        xor rax, rax;
        mov [rdi + 0], rax;

        // ids1 ([+8]): seat_id[31:0] | keyboard[63:32].
        // Mask seat_id to low 32 via shl 32 / shr 32.
        mov r10, rsi;
        shl r10, 32;
        shr r10, 32;
        // Move keyboard into high 32 bits.
        mov r11, rdx;
        shl r11, 32;
        or  r10, r11;
        mov [rdi + 8], r10;

        // ids2 ([+16]): pointer[31:0] | touch(=0)[63:32].
        mov r10, rcx;
        shl r10, 32;
        shr r10, 32;
        mov [rdi + 16], r10;

        // ids3 ([+24]): stylus(=0)[31:0] | active_workspace(=0)[63:32].
        xor rax, rax;
        mov [rdi + 24], rax;

        // state ([+32]): locked(=0) byte + reserved zero.
        mov [rdi + 32], rax;

        // Reserved words ([+40], [+48], [+56]) all zero.
        mov [rdi + 40], rax;
        mov [rdi + 48], rax;
        mov [rdi + 56], rax;

        // SEAT_OK = 0.
        xor rax, rax;
        ret;

      seat_m_bad_id:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFEB7E;    // SEAT_MINT_BAD_ID
        ret;
      seat_m_bad_kb:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFEB7D;    // SEAT_MINT_BAD_KEYBOARD
        ret;
      seat_m_bad_ptr:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFEB7C;    // SEAT_MINT_BAD_POINTER
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // SECTION 11 -- BIND: seat_bind_touch(row_ptr, touch_kind)
  // ==========================================================================
  //
  // Writes touch_kind_id into the high 32 bits of ids2 ([+16]),
  // preserving the low 32 bits (pointer_kind_id).
  //
  // Refuses:
  //   * touch_kind == 0 or upper 32 bits set   -> SEAT_BIND_BAD_ID
  //   * touch already bound (high 32 != 0)     -> SEAT_BIND_ALREADY
  //
  // Signature: (row_ptr: u64, touch_kind: u64) -> u64
  pub let seat_bind_touch : (u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (row_ptr: u64) (touch_kind: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "B8-06 (#2285): bind KIND_TOUCH_DEVICE to seat. Writes touch_kind into ids2[63:32] preserving pointer_kind_id at ids2[31:0]. Validates touch_kind nonzero + fits u32 (SEAT_BIND_BAD_ID) and refuses if the slot is already occupied (SEAT_BIND_ALREADY -- once-only binding, a device swap requires revoke first). All u32 masking via shl/shr 32. Scratch: rax, r8, r9, r10, r11. Labels prefixed seat_bt_.",
      block: {
        // Validate touch_kind nonzero + fits u32.
        cmp rsi, 0;
        je  seat_bt_bad;
        mov r10, rsi;
        shr r10, 32;
        cmp r10, 0;
        jne seat_bt_bad;

        // Load current ids2, check high 32 == 0.
        mov r8, [rdi + 16];
        mov r10, r8;
        shr r10, 32;
        cmp r10, 0;
        jne seat_bt_already;

        // Mask low 32 of r8 (pointer_kind_id) via shl/shr 32.
        shl r8, 32;
        shr r8, 32;
        // Move touch_kind into high 32.
        mov r11, rsi;
        shl r11, 32;
        or  r8, r11;
        mov [rdi + 16], r8;

        xor rax, rax;           // SEAT_OK
        ret;

      seat_bt_bad:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFEB7A;    // SEAT_BIND_BAD_ID
        ret;
      seat_bt_already:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFEB79;    // SEAT_BIND_ALREADY
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // SECTION 12 -- BIND: seat_bind_stylus(row_ptr, stylus_kind)
  // ==========================================================================
  //
  // Writes stylus_kind_id into the low 32 bits of ids3 ([+24]),
  // preserving the high 32 bits (active_workspace_kind_id).
  //
  // Refuses:
  //   * stylus_kind == 0 or upper 32 bits set  -> SEAT_BIND_BAD_ID
  //   * stylus already bound (low 32 != 0)     -> SEAT_BIND_ALREADY
  //
  // Signature: (row_ptr: u64, stylus_kind: u64) -> u64
  pub let seat_bind_stylus : (u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (row_ptr: u64) (stylus_kind: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "B8-06 (#2285): bind KIND_STYLUS_DEVICE to seat. Writes stylus_kind into ids3[31:0] preserving active_workspace_kind_id at ids3[63:32]. Validates stylus_kind nonzero + fits u32 (SEAT_BIND_BAD_ID) and refuses if the slot is already occupied (SEAT_BIND_ALREADY). All u32 masking via shl/shr 32. Labels prefixed seat_bs_.",
      block: {
        // Validate stylus_kind nonzero + fits u32.
        cmp rsi, 0;
        je  seat_bs_bad;
        mov r10, rsi;
        shr r10, 32;
        cmp r10, 0;
        jne seat_bs_bad;

        // Load current ids3, check low 32 == 0.
        mov r8, [rdi + 24];
        mov r10, r8;
        shl r10, 32;
        shr r10, 32;
        cmp r10, 0;
        jne seat_bs_already;

        // r8 currently holds active_workspace_kind_id in the high 32
        // (low 32 is zero per the "already-zero" check). OR in stylus
        // as the new low 32.
        mov r11, rsi;
        shl r11, 32;
        shr r11, 32;    // stylus_kind masked to low 32
        or  r8, r11;
        mov [rdi + 24], r8;

        xor rax, rax;           // SEAT_OK
        ret;

      seat_bs_bad:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFEB7A;    // SEAT_BIND_BAD_ID
        ret;
      seat_bs_already:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFEB79;    // SEAT_BIND_ALREADY
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // SECTION 13 -- LOCK: seat_lock(row_ptr) / seat_unlock(row_ptr)
  // ==========================================================================
  //
  // The two capability operations that mediate screen-lock. Both
  // write the low byte of state ([+32]); the dispatch check is a
  // single byte load against this same offset (P2 mitigation --
  // events dropped AT THE SEAT, not at every subscriber).
  //
  // seat_lock refuses SEAT_LOCK_ALREADY if the seat is already locked
  // (idempotence is a rights-holding concern; a locked seat must not
  // silently re-arm its "just locked" event stream).
  //
  // seat_unlock refuses SEAT_UNLOCK_ALREADY if the seat is already
  // unlocked. Same rationale.
  //
  // Signature (both): (row_ptr: u64) -> u64

  pub let seat_lock : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (row_ptr: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "B8-06 (#2285): transition locked 0 -> 1. Reads state word at [+32], AND with 0xFF to isolate the locked byte, refuses SEAT_LOCK_ALREADY if it is already 1. Otherwise writes state word with locked byte = 1 preserving reserved bits. Labels prefixed seat_lk_.",
      block: {
        mov r8, [rdi + 32];
        mov r10, r8;
        and r10, 0xFF;
        cmp r10, 1;
        je  seat_lk_already;

        // Clear low byte, then OR in 1. We use `mov r11, 0xFF; not r11`
        // to build the ~0xFF mask via a small imm rather than
        // `and reg, 0xFFFFFF00` -- same posture xdg_shell_states.pdx
        // uses for its state_pack byte clear.
        mov r11, 0xFF;
        not r11;                 // r11 = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFF00
        and r8, r11;
        mov r10, 1;
        or  r8, r10;
        mov [rdi + 32], r8;

        xor rax, rax;            // SEAT_OK
        ret;

      seat_lk_already:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFEB78;     // SEAT_LOCK_ALREADY
        ret
      }
    }

  pub let seat_unlock : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (row_ptr: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "B8-06 (#2285): transition locked 1 -> 0. Reads state word at [+32], AND with 0xFF to isolate the locked byte, refuses SEAT_UNLOCK_ALREADY if it is already 0. Otherwise writes state word with locked byte cleared, preserving reserved bits. Labels prefixed seat_ul_.",
      block: {
        mov r8, [rdi + 32];
        mov r10, r8;
        and r10, 0xFF;
        cmp r10, 0;
        je  seat_ul_already;

        // Clear low byte (leaves locked = 0).
        mov r11, 0xFF;
        not r11;                 // r11 = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFF00
        and r8, r11;
        mov [rdi + 32], r8;

        xor rax, rax;            // SEAT_OK
        ret;

      seat_ul_already:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFEB77;     // SEAT_UNLOCK_ALREADY
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // SECTION 14 -- QUERIES
  // ==========================================================================

  // seat_is_locked(row_ptr) -> u64 (0 or 1).
  // Reads the state word at [+32] and masks to the low byte. This is
  // the DISPATCH-GATE READ: the input server's event fanout loop
  // consults this on every event batch. Kept minimal (one load, one
  // and, one ret) so the check adds sub-cycle overhead to the hot
  // path.
  pub let seat_is_locked : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (row_ptr: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "B8-06 (#2285): dispatch-gate predicate. Returns 1 iff the low byte of state ([+32]) is 1. One load, one AND with imm8 0xFF (small immediate, no encoder concern), one ret. Labels prefixed seat_il_.",
      block: {
        mov rax, [rdi + 32];
        and rax, 0xFF;
        ret
      }
    }

  // seat_active_workspace(row_ptr) -> u32 (as u64).
  // Reads the high 32 bits of ids3 ([+24]) via shr 32.
  pub let seat_active_workspace : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (row_ptr: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "B8-06 (#2285): read active_workspace_kind_id from ids3[63:32]. One load, one shr 32, one ret. Labels prefixed seat_aw_.",
      block: {
        mov rax, [rdi + 24];
        shr rax, 32;
        ret
      }
    }
}
